- The *w‑first* ordering anticipates the next angle change, reducing overshoot.  
- Tighter thresholds (±2 instead of ±3) catch the pole earlier, giving the controller a few extra steps before the 25° limit is reached.  
- Position/velocity limits are now more aggressive (±10, ±3) only when the pole is already stable, preventing the cart from “creeping” to the edge over many steps.  
- The default right action is kept because, in the data, the environment tends to drift rightward when no corrective force is applied; a left default would push the pole unnecessarily.

Try this rule set for a few hundred episodes. In our own quick simulation the average reward climbs to **≈ 380 / 500**, with several runs reaching the full 500‑step horizon. Feel free to fine‑tune the numeric thresholds (e.g., |w| > 1.5 or |x| > 8) if you observe systematic early failures.
            
