## Updated Strategy (Policy C)

The new rule set keeps the same simple “if‑then‑else” structure but changes the ordering and adds a *quiet‑zone* check for the cart‑position.

```
# Variables:  x = cart position
#             v = cart velocity
#             a = pole angle   (positive = pole leaning right)
#             w = pole angular velocity (positive = rotating clockwise)

IF |a| >= 3 THEN                     # strong tilt → push in direction of tilt
    IF a > 0 THEN action = 2        # pole leaning right → push right
    ELSE        action = 1          # pole leaning left  → push left
ELSE IF |w| >= 3 THEN                # fast rotation → push in direction of rotation
    IF w > 0 THEN action = 2
    ELSE        action = 1
ELSE IF |x| >= 22 AND |a| <= 1 AND |w| <= 1 THEN   # cart near border, pole almost upright
    IF x > 0 THEN action = 1        # cart too far right → push left
    ELSE        action = 2          # cart too far left  → push right
ELSE IF |v| >= 5 THEN                # cart moving fast, pole still near vertical
    IF v > 0 THEN action = 1        # moving right → push left
    ELSE        action = 2          # moving left  → push right
ELSE                                   # everything is small → keep cart centred
    action = 2                         # default to a small right push (breaks symmetry)
```

### Why this should work better

| Situation | Decision rule | Reasoning |
|-----------|---------------|-----------|
| **Large tilt** (|a|≥3) | Rule 1 | Directly counteracts the torque; the sign of the angle tells which side the pole is falling. |
| **Fast rotation** (|w|≥3) | Rule 2 | When the pole is already near vertical but rotating quickly, the sign of *w* tells which direction the base must move to damp the rotation. |
| **Cart close to the track limits** (|x|≥22) **and** pole almost upright (|a|,|w|≤1) | Rule 3 | Only now do we care about the cart’s location; otherwise a position push would disturb a still‑balancing pole. |
| **Cart moving quickly** (|v|≥5) **and** pole still near vertical | Rule 4 | A fast‑moving cart will soon hit a wall; a gentle opposite push reduces kinetic energy without upsetting the pole. |
| **All variables small** | Rule 5 | Keep a tiny bias to the right (action = 2) to avoid getting stuck in a dead‑lock; any other constant would work equally. |

The thresholds (3 for angle/velocity, 22 for position, 5 for cart speed) are the same numbers that already gave the best empirical performance, only the **order** and the **extra “pole‑quiet” guard on the position rule** have changed.  

---

