| **S‑2** (more balanced but still ad‑hoc) | **387.1** | Introduced a dedicated pole‑angle rule (|a| ≥ 3) and used the cart‑velocity when the pole was near‑vertical. This delayed the crash. | The “else‑default = right” bias pushed the cart continuously to the right once the pole entered the small‑angle region, so the cart eventually hit the +25 limit. |
| **S‑3** (the “best‑so‑far” rule set) | **482.4** | • Pole‑angle ≥ 3 and angular‑velocity ≥ 3 are handled first – the cart pushes in the same direction the pole leans/rotates, which is the classic linear‑controller intuition. <br>• When the pole is almost upright (|a|,|w| ≤ 1) we start correcting the cart position (|x| ≥ 22). <br>• Cart‑velocity (|v| ≥ 5) is used as a secondary safety net. <br>These three layers keep the pole upright for > 450 steps in almost every run. | The “default = right” (action = 2) is still present. When the pole finally crosses the 22°‑25° window the controller keeps pushing right, which is the wrong sign for the last few steps (see the switch from action = 2 → 1 at step 12). This is why the episode still ends before the full 500 steps. |

