    IF w > 0 THEN ACTION = 2        # clockwise rotation → push right
    ELSE      ACTION = 1            # counter‑clockwise → push left
ELSE IF |x| >= 22 THEN               # cart is getting close to the edge
    IF x > 0 THEN ACTION = 1        # too far right → push left
    ELSE      ACTION = 2            # too far left  → push right
ELSE IF |v| >= 5 THEN                # cart moving fast towards an edge
    IF v > 0 THEN ACTION = 1        # moving right → push left
    ELSE      ACTION = 2            # moving left  → push right
ELSE                                 # everything quiet → small bias right
    ACTION = 2
END IF
```

#### Why this version should be even more reliable

| Modification | Reason |
|--------------|--------|
| **Keep the angle threshold at 3** (instead of 2) | Prevents the controller from reacting to tiny, harmless tilts that would otherwise trigger unnecessary pushes. |
| **Raise angular‑velocity dead‑band to 3** | In the best run the controller switched to w‑control a little *too early* (|w| = 2) and kept pushing left while the pole was already close to the –25° limit. By requiring |w| ≥ 3 we stay in the safe “angle‑dominant” mode a bit longer, giving the pole more time to settle. |
| **Add explicit cart‑position safety (|x| ≥ 22)** | The original S‑3 never used this clause, but it protects against rare drift that could push the cart out of bounds before the pole falls. |
| **Keep the default “push right”** | Empirically the right‑bias yields the longest episodes; it aligns with the natural clockwise drift that appears when the pole is almost upright. |
| **All thresholds are integer‑friendly** | The environment only supplies integer values, so the rules are deterministic and cheap to evaluate. |

---

### 4. Expected behaviour (step‑by‑step)

1. **Large lean (|a| ≥ 3)** → push in the same direction → the cart accelerates under the pole, creating a restoring torque.  
2. **Pole almost upright, rotating (|w| ≥ 3)** → push in the *same* direction as the rotation → kinetic energy of the rotation is dissipated by the cart’s motion.  
3. **Cart drifting toward a wall (|x| ≥ 22) or moving fast (|v| ≥ 5)** → override everything and push back toward centre – this only activates in the very last few steps of a trial, so it never fights the pole‑stabilising pushes.  
4. **All quiet** → a tiny right nudge keeps the system biased toward the stable clockwise rotation that naturally appears when the pole is balanced.

---

