| Cart‑edge hits           | ~2 per 20 trials  | ≤ 1 (position rule tighter) |
| Need for “default‑right” | occasional       | only at start, then stable |

The improvement comes mainly from **always damping the pole’s spin before trying to correct the angle**. In the few cases where the angle is already huge (|θ| ≥ 22) we still give it priority, because a single large corrective push is the only thing that can keep the pole from crossing ±25°.

### 5. What to try next  

1. **Run the new hierarchy for at least 30 trials** and record the reward list.  
2. If the average climbs above 480, consider a *soft‑threshold* for ω (e.g., |ω| ≥ 2.5) to start damping a little earlier.  
3. Optionally, add a **tiny “hold” action** (no push) when |θ| < 1 and |ω| < 1 – this may reduce unnecessary jitter.  

---

**Bottom line:**  
The previous best policy was already strong, but it occasionally amplified the pole’s rotation because it ignored the sign of angular velocity when the angle was also large. By moving the ω‑damping rule ahead of the moderate‑angle rule (and tightening the cart‑position threshold) we obtain a cleaner, more reliable controller that should push the average episode length well beyond the current 445‑step benchmark. Feel free to copy the pseudo‑code above into the next experiment run. Good luck!
