### 2. Re‑formulated rule set  

Below is a compact, deterministic policy that reproduces the successful ordering while being easier to read.  
All variables are integers in `[-50, 50]`.  
`ACTION = 1` → push **left** (negative force)  
`ACTION = 2` → push **right** (positive force)

```text
# -------------------------------------------------
# 1️⃣  BIG ANGLE – immediate correction
# -------------------------------------------------
IF |a| >= 22:
    ACTION = 1  IF a > 0  ELSE 2                # pole far from vertical → push opposite side

# -------------------------------------------------
# 2️⃣  MEDIUM ANGLE – still need opposite side push
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |a| >= 3:
    ACTION = 2  IF a > 0  ELSE 1                # pole tilted moderately → push opposite side

# -------------------------------------------------
# 3️⃣  SMALL ANGLE – use angular velocity to decide
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |a| <= 2 AND w != 0:
    ACTION = 2  IF w > 0  ELSE 1                # pole near upright, but spinning → counter‑spin

# -------------------------------------------------
# 4️⃣  SPINNING FAST – still correct the rotation
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |w| >= 3:
    ACTION = 2  IF w > 0  ELSE 1                # strong spin, regardless of angle

# -------------------------------------------------
# 5️⃣  CART POSITION – keep cart inside track
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |x| >= 18:
    ACTION = 1  IF x > 0  ELSE 2                # cart too far right → push left, etc.

# -------------------------------------------------
# 6️⃣  CART VELOCITY – damp cart motion when everything else is quiet
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |v| >= 2:
    ACTION = 1  IF v > 0  ELSE 2                # cart moving away from centre → push opposite

# -------------------------------------------------
# 7️⃣  DEFAULT – fall back to opposite‑side of tiny tilt
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE:
    ACTION = 1  IF a > 0  ELSE 2                # tiny tilt → push opposite side
```

**Why this works**

| Rule | Physical intuition | Why it prevents failure |
|------|--------------------|--------------------------|
| 1 & 2 (|a| ≥ 3) | Angle dominates the dynamics – a large tilt creates a torque that will quickly drive the pole out of the safe band unless a force is applied on the *opposite* side. | Immediate counter‑torque stops the pole from crossing ±25°. |
| 3 (|a| ≤ 2 & w ≠ 0) | When the pole is almost upright, the only thing that can make it fall is angular momentum. | Applying a force opposite the direction of rotation creates a restoring torque that damps the spin. |
| 4 (|w| ≥ 3) | Even if the angle is exactly zero, a fast spin will soon generate a large angle (because `a` integrates `w`). | Counter‑spin before the angle grows. |
| 5 (|x| ≥ 18) | The cart itself must stay inside the track; once the pole is under control, we can safely move the cart back toward the centre. | Prevents the secondary failure mode (cart hitting the track edge). |
| 6 (|v| ≥ 2) | A fast‑moving cart will overshoot the centre and may later force the pole toward a limit. | Damps cart velocity without interfering with pole‑control rules. |
| 7 (fallback) | Handles the rare case where everything is near zero but a tiny tilt remains. | Guarantees a deterministic decision. |

