**2️⃣ Updated rule set (the “Effective‑Balance” policy)**  

```text
# thresholds (tuned on the integer‑range environment)
TH_ANGLE   = 3      # |θ|   – when the pole is noticeably tilted
TH_OMEGA   = 2      # |ω|   – when the pole is rotating fast
TH_POS     = 18     # |x|   – when the cart is close to the track edge
TH_VEL     = 2      # |v|   – when the cart is moving fast

# --------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Strong tilt – act opposite to the tilt
IF |θ| ≥ TH_ANGLE THEN
    IF θ > 0 THEN action = 2      # pole tilts right → push right
    ELSE       action = 1         # pole tilts left  → push left

# 2. Fast rotation – act opposite to the angular velocity
ELSE IF |ω| ≥ TH_OMEGA THEN
    IF ω > 0 THEN action = 1      # pole rotating right → push left
    ELSE       action = 2         # pole rotating left  → push right

# 3. Cart near the limits – drive it back to centre
ELSE IF |x| ≥ TH_POS THEN
    IF x > 0 THEN action = 1      # cart far right → push left
    ELSE       action = 2         # cart far left  → push right

# 4. Cart moving fast – damp the velocity
ELSE IF |v| ≥ TH_VEL THEN
    IF v > 0 THEN action = 1      # cart moving right → push left
    ELSE       action = 2         # cart moving left  → push right

# 5. Fine‑tune when everything is small
ELSE
    IF θ ≠ 0 THEN                 # tiny tilt – correct it
        IF θ > 0 THEN action = 2
        ELSE       action = 1
    ELSE
        action = 1                # arbitrary default (left)
```

**Why this works**

* The **first two clauses** are the only ones that matter for the majority of the episode – they keep the pole from falling by always applying a force that *reduces* the dominant error (angle or angular speed).  
* The **position/velocity clauses** are safety guards; they are only triggered when the cart is drifting toward the failure limits. They prevent the secondary failure mode (cart runs off the track) without interfering with pole‑stabilisation.  
* The **fine‑tune clause** removes the last few degrees of tilt when the pole is already almost vertical, giving the controller a “bias‑toward‑centre” that helps reach the full 500‑step horizon.

