The pattern shows two simple, robust principles:

1. **When the pole is noticeably tilted (|θ| ≥ 3°) we always push *toward* the side the pole is leaning** – this creates a restoring torque.  
2. **If the pole is almost upright but spinning fast (|θ| < 3° & |ω| ≥ 2) we push *in the direction of the spin* to cancel the angular velocity.**  

Cart‑position (`x`) and cart‑velocity (`v`) are only needed as *last‑resort* safety nets (when both |θ| and |ω| are small) to keep the cart from hitting the walls. In the successful runs the cart never left the interval `[-18, 18]`, so those safety rules were rarely triggered.

