## Updated Rule Set (final strategy)

```
# -------------------------------------------------
# 1. Primary: pole angle (θ)
# -------------------------------------------------
IF |θ| > 3                     # pole noticeably leaning
    ACTION = (θ > 0) ? 2 : 1   # push in the direction of the lean

# -------------------------------------------------
# 2. Secondary: angular velocity (ω) – only when angle is small
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |ω| > 2                # pole rotating fast
    ACTION = (ω > 0) ? 2 : 1   # push in the direction of the rotation
                                 # (this opposes the rotation)

# -------------------------------------------------
# 3. Tertiary: cart position (x) – far from centre
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |x| > 18               # cart drifting towards a wall
    ACTION = (x > 0) ? 1 : 2   # push back towards the centre

# -------------------------------------------------
# 4. Quaternary: cart velocity (v) – moving fast away from centre
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE IF |v| > 2                # cart sliding quickly
    ACTION = (v > 0) ? 1 : 2   # push opposite to the velocity

# -------------------------------------------------
# 5. Default (all signals tiny) – keep the pole upright
# -------------------------------------------------
ELSE
    ACTION = (θ > 0) ? 2 : 1   # if the pole is even slightly leaning,
                               # push in that direction; otherwise push left
```

### Why this works

| Rule | Reason |
|------|--------|
| **Angle > 3** | The lean is the earliest indicator of a fall; correcting it directly neutralises the torque before the angular velocity grows. |
| **|ω| > 2** (when angle is small) | Even a near‑vertical pole can tip over if it spins fast; a push that follows the rotation quickly damps ω and prevents the angle from blowing up. |
| **|x| > 18** | The cart is close to the edge of the allowed track (±25).  A gentle nudge back avoids the “cart‑out‑of‑bounds” failure mode. |
| **|v| > 2** | A fast‑moving cart can overshoot the centre after a large correction; pushing opposite to its velocity re‑centres it without overshoot. |
| **Default** | When everything is already tiny the system is effectively balanced; a small push in the direction of the tiny residual lean keeps the pole centred without inducing unnecessary oscillations. |

The thresholds (3° for angle, 2 units for ω, 18 units for x, 2 units for v) are exactly the ones that produced the 500‑step runs in the previous experiments, so we keep them unchanged.  The ordering guarantees that the *most urgent* signal (angle) always wins, followed by the next‑most‑urgent (angular velocity), and only when both are calm do we worry about the cart’s position or speed.

---

