### 3️⃣  Why this works  

| Rule | Physical intuition | Expected effect |
|------|--------------------|-----------------|
| **1. Angle** | A tilted pole creates a torque that will make it fall in the same direction unless the cart moves under the base of the pole. | Pushing **toward** the tilt moves the cart under the pole’s centre of mass, reducing the angle. |
| **2. Angular velocity** | A rotating pole will keep rotating; moving the cart in the same direction adds a counter‑torque that damps the rotation. | The pole’s angular speed is reduced, preventing it from overshooting the vertical. |
| **3. Position** | When the cart is near the track limits, any further motion in that direction would cause a failure. | The cart is driven back toward the centre, keeping enough margin for later corrections. |
| **4. Velocity** | If the cart is already drifting outward, we must brake that drift. | The cart’s drift is arrested before it reaches the position limit. |
| **5. Default** | When everything is already small, the safest “do‑something” is to keep the pole centred. | Prevents the controller from becoming idle and lets the pole stay within the ±1° band that the previous steps have shown to be stable. |

### 4️⃣  Next steps / possible refinements  

